  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Hydration measurement pilot</p>
    <h2>A useful hydration study should not depend on one number</h2>
    <p>Potential pilot measures include urine osmolality, urine specific gravity and urine volume, combined with contextual measures such as fluid intake, timing, body mass, thirst or other clinically appropriate variables. More advanced renal or metabolic biomarkers may be considered only when they fit a clearly defined research question and protocol.</p>
    <p>The literature is clear that urine concentration reflects renal water conservation and is affected by timing, diet, prior fluid intake, illness and other factors. Spot urine values therefore need cautious interpretation, especially when used alone.</p>
    <p>For that reason, our planned measurement work is framed around a <strong>panel</strong> and within-person context rather than a simplistic “one cutoff equals dehydrated” model.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Measurement principle</p>
    <h2>Hydration status is estimated, not directly captured by a universal field test</h2>
    <p>A 2020 review in the Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition concluded that no single hydration assessment is without limitations and recommended combining complementary measures. Urine specific gravity and osmolality can be useful, but their interpretation depends on sampling method, timing and context.</p>
    <p>That makes study design as important as the biomarker itself. A protocol should state when samples are collected, what participants consumed, what comparison is being made and what the measurement can and cannot represent.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Clinical development</p>
    <h2>Observational work should define questions before intervention</h2>
    <p>Clinical hydration is not one problem. Age, illness, medication, kidney function, gastrointestinal conditions, fluid losses and care setting can all change what a meaningful hydration outcome looks like.</p>
    <p>Early non-interventional or observational work can be useful for understanding workflows, feasible measurements, baseline variability and clinically relevant endpoints before a product intervention is justified.</p>
    <p>Any human-participant study must be governed by the applicable research-ethics, consent, privacy and institutional requirements. This page does not imply that an exploratory protocol has ethics approval, has recruited participants or has produced results unless that status is explicitly stated.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Clinical boundary</span>
    <h3>Research participation and medical care are different relationships.</h3>
    <p>A research protocol should never be presented as individualized diagnosis, treatment or a substitute for a participant's clinical care.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Before a project becomes “active”</p>
  <h2>Minimum research gates</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__steps">
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Question and protocol</h3><p>Define population, formulation or exposure, comparator where relevant, timing, outcomes, analysis plan and stopping or safety considerations.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Governance and approvals</h3><p>Confirm ethics review, informed consent, privacy, data handling, investigator roles and any regulatory requirements that apply to the project.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Feasibility and measurement quality</h3><p>Confirm that collection methods, equipment, sampling windows and participant burden can produce interpretable data.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Transparent status reporting</h3><p>Only then move the project from planned to active, and later to completed, analyzed and published as each milestone is actually reached.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

